IP throttling is a technique used to limit the number of requests that can come from a single IP address within a certain time period.
It is mainly used to protect applications from abuse, excessive traffic, and attacks.

Why do we use IP throttling?
Prevent DDoS attacks
Stop brute-force login attempts
Prevent API abuse
Ensure fair usage among users
Protect server resources and improve performance
Example

Suppose your login API allows:

5 login attempts per minute per IP

If an IP address (192.168.1.10) sends:

Request 1 ✅
Request 2 ✅
Request 3 ✅
Request 4 ✅
Request 5 ✅
Request 6 ❌ (Blocked - Too Many Requests)

The server returns:

HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests

Sometimes it also sends a header like:

Retry-After: 60

which means the client should wait 60 seconds before trying again.

How it works
Client (IP: 203.0.113.5)
        |
        | 100 requests
        |
API Gateway / Load Balancer
        |
   Check IP Counter
        |
   <= 50 requests/min ? ---- Yes ----> Allow
        |
        No
        |
Return 429 Too Many Requests


Common Algorithms
1. Fixed Window
Example: 100 requests per minute.
Counter resets every minute.
Simple but can allow bursts at window boundaries.
2. Sliding Window
Uses the last 60 seconds continuously.
More accurate and smoother.
3. Token Bucket
Tokens are added at a fixed rate.
Each request consumes one token.
Allows occasional bursts.
4. Leaky Bucket
Requests are processed at a constant rate.
Extra requests wait or are dropped.
In Spring Boot

Using Spring Cloud Gateway:

spring:
  cloud:
    gateway:
      routes:
      - id: api
        uri: http://localhost:8080
        predicates:
        - Path=/api/**
        filters:
        - name: RequestRateLimiter
          args:
            redis-rate-limiter.replenishRate: 10
            redis-rate-limiter.burstCapacity: 20

Here:

replenishRate: 10 → 10 new requests allowed per second.
burstCapacity: 20 → Up to 20 requests can be handled in a burst.

Redis is commonly used to keep request counters across multiple application instances.

IP Throttling vs User Throttling
| IP Throttling                                 | User Throttling                 |
| --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| Limits by IP address                          | Limits by authenticated user ID |
| Good before login                             | Good after login                |
| Can affect multiple users behind the same NAT | Fair for individual users       |
| Easier to bypass using different IPs          | Harder to bypass                |



Interview Answer (30 seconds)

IP throttling is a rate-limiting mechanism that restricts how many requests a client can make based on its IP address within a specific time window.
It helps prevent brute-force attacks, DDoS attacks, and API abuse while ensuring fair resource usage. When the limit is exceeded,
the server typically responds with HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests). Common implementations use algorithms like Token Bucket,
Sliding Window, or Leaky Bucket, often with Redis to store request counts in distributed systems.